## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

**Summary: 10 tests, 12 findings (5 Critical, 1 High, 1 Medium, 1 Low)**

This suite is a textbook **coverage-touching** pattern. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called, which gets you to ~95% line coverage, but almost nothing is actually *verified*. Bugs sail through because these tests cannot fail when behavior breaks.

---

### 🔴 Critical — False Confidence

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (systemic)
**Lines 17–89 — entire class**

All 10 tests methodically call every public method in declaration order with names like `Xxx_Works`. 5 of 10 have zero meaningful assertions. The remaining 5 use only `Assert.IsNotNull`, which proves the method returned *something* but not the *right* thing. This is the root cause of your production bugs.

#### 2. No Assertions — `AddProduct_Works` (line 17)
Calls `AddProduct` but never checks the product was persisted.
```csharp
// BEFORE (line 17-21)
public void AddProduct_Works()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);
}

// AFTER
public void AddProduct_StoresProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);
    var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored?.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored?.Price);
}
```

#### 3. No Assertions — `UpdatePrice_Works` (line 32)
Calls `UpdatePrice` but never checks the price changed. The `UpdatePrice` method has a `KeyNotFoundException` path (line 14 of production code) — that's also untested.
```csharp
// AFTER
public void UpdatePrice_UpdatesStoredPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
    var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated?.Price);
}

public void UpdatePrice_NonexistentSku_ThrowsKeyNotFound()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<KeyNotFoundException>(
        () => _catalog.UpdatePrice("MISSING", 1m));
}
```

#### 4. No Assertions — `RemoveProduct_Works` (line 38)
Calls `RemoveProduct` but never verifies the product is gone.
```csharp
// AFTER
public void RemoveProduct_ProductNoLongerRetrievable()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}
